// synth: library theory (phase 0)
// doc: In Python, filter(function, iterable) returns an iterator yielding, in
// doc: the same order as the iterable, exactly those elements x of iterable
// doc: for which function(x) is truthy. If function is None, elements that are
// doc: truthy are yielded. The iterable is not mutated by filter. Converting
// doc: that iterator to a list (via list(...)) produces a list with those
// doc: elements in the same order. In this theory we model the common case used
// doc: in the program: filtering a sequence of integers with a boolean-valued
// doc: predicate and eagerly returning a sequence of ints preserving order.
// doc: The modeled function is total and deterministic and does not raise
// doc: exceptions; Python itself returns a lazy iterator and only raises on
// doc: unrelated errors (e.g. if the predicate itself raises).
function FilterInt(pred: int -> bool, s: seq<int>): seq<int>
  decreases |s|
{
  if |s| == 0 then []
  else if pred(s[0]) then [s[0]] + FilterInt(pred, s[1..])
  else FilterInt(pred, s[1..])
}

