// doc: In Python, max(iterable) returns the largest element of the iterable according
// doc: to Python's element ordering (for floats, the usual numeric ordering).
// doc: If the iterable is empty, Python raises ValueError. For a non-empty finite
// doc: sequence, the result is some element s[i] such that for all j, s[i] >= s[j].
// doc: This function models that behaviour for a finite Dafny sequence of reals.
// doc: It requires the sequence to be non-empty (to represent Python's ValueError
// doc: when called on an empty iterable).
function Py_max(s: seq<real>): real
  requires |s| > 0
  decreases |s|
{
  if |s| == 1 then s[0]
  else var tailMax := Py_max(s[1..]);
       if s[0] >= tailMax then s[0] else tailMax
}

// doc: In Python, min(iterable) returns the smallest element of the iterable
// doc: according to Python's element ordering (for floats, the usual numeric ordering).
// doc: If the iterable is empty, Python raises ValueError. For a non-empty finite
// doc: sequence, the result is some element s[i] such that for all j, s[i] <= s[j].
// doc: This function models that behaviour for a finite Dafny sequence of reals.
// doc: It requires the sequence to be non-empty (to represent Python's ValueError
// doc: when called on an empty iterable).
function Py_min(s: seq<real>): real
  requires |s| > 0
  decreases |s|
{
  if |s| == 1 then s[0]
  else var tailMin := Py_min(s[1..]);
       if s[0] <= tailMin then s[0] else tailMin
}

// doc: In Python, the binary operator '/' performs true division. When both operands
// doc: are floats, the result is a float equal to mathematical division a/b.
// doc: If the right-hand operand is zero (exactly 0.0), Python raises ZeroDivisionError.
// doc: This function models Python's float division for Dafny reals and requires the
// doc: divisor to be non-zero to represent the ZeroDivisionError case.
function Py_div(a: real, b: real): real
  requires b != 0.0
{
  a / b
}

// doc: In Python 3, map(func, iterable) returns an iterator which yields func(x)
// doc: for each element x of the iterable, in the iterable's iteration order.
// doc: When that iterator is consumed to build a list (list(map(...))), the result
// doc: is a list containing the values func(x) for each element x of the original
// doc: iterable, in the same order. If func raises an exception for some element,
// doc: that exception is raised at the time that element is evaluated during iteration.
// doc: This function models the fully-evaluated list(list(map(func, seq))) behavior
// doc: for the common case where the iterable is a finite sequence of floats and func
// doc: is a pure callable from float to float: it returns the sequence of f(s[i])
// doc: for i = 0..|s|-1, preserving order.
function Py_map_float(f: real -> real, s: seq<real>): seq<real>
  decreases |s|
{
  if |s| == 0 then [] else [f(s[0])] + Py_map_float(f, s[1..])
}

// doc: In Python, list(iterable) constructs a new list containing all the elements
// doc: produced by iterating over iterable, in iteration order. If iterable is already
// doc: a list, the result is a shallow copy (a new list object with the same elements
// doc: in the same order). This function models that behaviour for finite sequences
// doc: of floats by returning a new sequence with the same elements in the same order.
function Py_list_from_seq(s: seq<real>): seq<real>
{
  // Model Python's list(...) on a finite sequence as an identity on values
  // but conceptually producing a distinct list object; here we return the same
  // sequence of values (Dafny sequences are immutable values).
  s
}

