// synth: library theory (phase 0)
// doc: Python list objects are ordered, indexable, and mutable sequences.
// doc: Indexing with a non-negative integer i returns the element at position i
// doc: (0-based). Indexing with a negative integer -k returns the element at
// doc: position len(list)-k. If an index (after negative-index adjustment) is
// doc: out of range, Python raises IndexError. Slicing list[start:end] produces a
// doc: new list containing elements from start (inclusive) to end (exclusive),
// doc: where omitted start/end are interpreted as the sequence start/end, and
// doc: negative indices are interpreted relative to the sequence length. Slicing
// doc: never raises IndexError; out-of-range slice bounds are clipped.
// doc: Many list operations (append, pop, in-place assignment to slices, etc.)
// doc: mutate the list in place. For the purposes of this theory we model the
// doc: contents of a Python list at a point in time as an immutable Dafny
// doc: datatype carrying the sequence of elements; this lets pure functions and
// doc: other specifications refer to the list contents as a value. This model
// doc: does not itself implement mutation semantics (mutating methods are not
// doc: provided here).
datatype PyList<T> = PyList(elems: seq<T>)

// doc: The Python builtin list() when given an iterable returns a new list
// doc: containing the elements of the iterable in iteration order. If the
// doc: iterable is empty, an empty list is returned. If evaluating the iterable
// doc: or any element raises an exception, that exception propagates and no
// doc: list object is produced. This function models converting a sequence of
// doc: elements into a Python list object with the same elements and order.
function py_list_from_seq<T>(s: seq<T>): PyList<T>
{
  PyList(s)
}

// doc: The effect of Python's filter(predicate, iterable) is to produce an
// doc: iterator (or, when consumed into a list, a sequence) of those elements e
// doc: drawn from the iterable in iteration order for which bool(predicate(e))
// doc: is true. If predicate(e) raises an exception for some element, that
// doc: exception propagates and iteration stops at that point. If predicate is
// doc: None, Python treats it as the identity function (keeping truthy elements);
// doc: this identity-None behaviour is not modeled here: a predicate must be
// doc: supplied. This function models filter by returning a Dafny sequence
// doc: containing the filtered elements in the same order they appeared in the
// doc: input sequence. It is total on its inputs (it does not itself raise), but
// doc: any exceptions raised by the predicate are not modeled and are assumed to
// doc: propagate in the Python implementation.
function py_filter_to_seq<T>(pred: T -> bool, it: seq<T>): seq<T>
  decreases |it|
{
  if |it| == 0 then
    []
  else
    if pred(it[0]) then
      [it[0]] + py_filter_to_seq(pred, it[1..])
    else
      py_filter_to_seq(pred, it[1..])
}

