// synth: library theory (phase 0)
// doc: In Python, a "list" is a mutable, ordered sequence of elements, supporting
// doc: indexing, iteration in increasing index order (from 0 to len-1), len(), and mutation.
// doc: For the purposes of reasoning about read-only operations (len, iteration, summation),
// doc: we model a Python list as an immutable Dafny sequence (seq<T>). This is an abstraction:
// doc: it preserves length, element order, and the results of iteration and indexing used by
// doc: read-only code. It does not model in-place mutation semantics.
type PyList<T> = seq<T>

// doc: Python's built-in len(x) returns the number of items of a sized object as a non-negative
// doc: integer. For lists, this is the number of elements. Python raises TypeError if the object
// doc: does not define a length; here we only provide len for PyList (Python lists).
function len_py<T>(l: PyList<T>): nat
{
  |l|
}

// doc: Python's built-in sum(iterable, start=0) returns the result of adding the items of the
// doc: iterable to the start value, using left-to-right binary +. For an empty iterable the
// doc: result is start. If elements are not summable (no + defined between them) Python raises
// doc: TypeError. When summing floats Python uses IEEE floating addition; here we model numeric
// doc: values as mathematical reals and addition as real addition. This function models sum(xs)
// doc: with start = 0 for a sequence of reals.
function sum_py(xs: seq<real>): real
  decreases |xs|
{
  if |xs| == 0 then 0.0 else xs[0] + sum_py(xs[1..])
}

// doc: A common Python idiom is a generator expression like (abs(x - mean) for x in numbers).
// doc: Such a generator iterates the source sequence in index order (0..len-1) and yields the
// doc: mapped values in that order. The function below models the sum of abs(x - mean) for each
// doc: x in the given sequence, i.e., it is equivalent to sum(abs(x - mean) for x in seq).
function sum_abs_diff(xs: seq<real>, mean: real): real
  decreases |xs|
{
  if |xs| == 0 then 0.0 else abs_py(xs[0] - mean) + sum_abs_diff(xs[1..], mean)
}

// doc: Python's built-in abs(x) for numeric x returns the non-negative magnitude of x.
// doc: For real (floating) inputs it yields the same signless magnitude; for NaN the result is NaN.
// doc: In this theory we model numeric values as mathematical reals (no NaN/Inf). For any real x,
// doc: abs(x) is x if x >= 0, and -x otherwise.
function abs_py(x: real): real
{
  if x >= 0.0 then x else -x
}

// doc: In Python 3 the operator "/" is true division: a / b returns a float (a real) equal to
// doc: the mathematical quotient. If b == 0.0 Python raises ZeroDivisionError. Here we model
// doc: true division on reals. To reflect that Python raises on zero divisor, this function has
// doc: a precondition requiring the divisor to be non-zero; callers must ensure that or the
// doc: corresponding Python code would raise ZeroDivisionError at runtime.
function py_truediv(a: real, b: real): real
  requires b != 0.0
{
  a / b
}

